<TopicPage slug="subjects/communication-studies/foundational/communication-theory">
  <h2>The idea</h2>
  <p>
    Communication theory asks the field's founding question: <em>what actually happens when
    people communicate?</em> The question looks naive until you try to answer it. Two friends
    talk and one goes home hurt by a sentence the other intended as a joke; a company's
    announcement calms half its staff and alarms the rest; a public health message saves lives
    in one community and hardens resistance in another. Whatever communication is, it is
    clearly not a parcel handed from one mind to another. The theories this page surveys form a
    spectrum of answers — from engineering's transmission pipes, through models of negotiated
    meaning, to the constitutive claim that our social realities are built <em>in</em>
    communication rather than merely reported by it. Knowing where a theory sits on that
    spectrum tells you what it can explain — and what it quietly assumes away.
  </p>
  <p>
    A running example will help: a manager tells a team, "the project has been deprioritised."
    Is that a message transmitted (did the words arrive intact?), a meaning negotiated (does
    "deprioritised" mean cancelled, postponed, or "the boss lost interest"?), an act that
    constitutes something (the team's status, the manager's authority, the trust in the room),
    or all three at once depending on which question you are asking? Communication theory is
    the discipline of keeping those questions distinct — and this page builds its three
    foundations: the models, the sign machinery, and the medium itself.
  </p>

  <Widget no="1" caption="from pipes to constitutive: the model spectrum" widget="equationStep" config={modelsCfg} />

  <h2>Models of communication: from pipe to transaction</h2>
  <p>
    The <strong>transmission model</strong> is the field's ancestor and its most useful
    villain. Claude Shannon's 1948 mathematical theory of communication — with Warren Weaver
    later popularising it — decomposed communication into a source, an encoder, a channel with
    noise, a decoder and a receiver, and asked a precise engineering question: how much
    information can pass a noisy channel, and how do encoding and redundancy defeat the noise?
    Its genius is real: bandwidth, bit rates, error-correcting codes and the entire digital
    infrastructure descend from it. Its trap is <em>metaphor creep</em>. The model was built for
    telephone signals, where "the message" is defined in advance and success means fidelity of
    reproduction. Apply it to humans and you get the <strong>conduit metaphor</strong> — minds
    as containers, meanings as objects, communication as mailing — which fails at every human
    subtlety. In our example, the words "deprioritised" arrived with perfect fidelity; the
    <em>meaning</em> went four directions at once. Lasswell's maxim ("who says what in which
    channel to whom with what effect?", 1948) improved the sociology but kept the plumbing.
  </p>
  <p>
    The <strong>meaning-centred models</strong> corrected the plumbing. Wilbur Schramm's
    insight was that sender and receiver each encode through their own <em>field of
    experience</em>, and communication only succeeds where the fields overlap — meaning is not
    transferred but negotiated across a gap. Osgood and Schramm circularised the model (no
    sender/receiver distinction; everyone encodes and decodes simultaneously), and the
    <strong>transactional models</strong> (Barnlund) went further: communication is
    simultaneous, multi-channel and context-saturated — posture, silence and seating carry
    "messages" nobody encoded deliberately, and the past relationship is present in every
    utterance. The <strong>constitutive view</strong>, crystallised in Robert Craig's
    "Communication Theory as a Field" (1999), makes the deepest move: communication does not
    merely report on a pre-existing social world — it <em>produces</em> that world. The team's
    morale, the manager's authority, even the organisation itself exist <em>in and through</em>
    the meetings, emails and silences; identity, relationship and institution are communication
    achievements, continuously maintained. Craig's map of the field's seven traditions
    (rhetorical, semiotic, cybernetic, socio-psychological, sociocultural, critical,
    phenomenological) is the discipline's own diagram of how many different questions "what
    happens when people communicate?" can be.
  </p>
  <p><em>Common misconception:</em> that communication succeeds when the message "gets through" — and that failure means someone transmitted badly. Under meaning-centred and constitutive models, success is <em>shared interpretation</em>, which is co-produced: the listener's frame, history and expectations are inside the loop. This is why "I told them" is not a defence — transmission is the cheapest part of communication, and often the least of it.</p>

  <h2>Semiotics: meaning lives in codes</h2>
  <p>
    If communication is negotiated meaning, semiotics is the study of what meaning is made
    <em>of</em>. Ferdinand de Saussure's founding distinction splits the <strong>sign</strong>
    into <em>signifier</em> (the sound, mark or image) and <em>signified</em> (the concept it
    evokes), and insists the bond between them is <strong>arbitrary and conventional</strong>:
    nothing about the sound "tree" is leafy, and nothing about a thumbs-up is approval —
    communities simply agree. The arbitrariness is observable: the same gesture flatters in
    one culture and insults in another; the same colour mourns in one country and celebrates
    in another; and the word for the same object differs across every language on Earth.
    Charles Sanders Peirce's parallel system classifies signs by how they signify —
    <strong>icons</strong> resemble (a photograph, a map), <strong>indices</strong> point
    (smoke means fire, a fever means infection), <strong>symbols</strong> depend on convention
    (words, flags, most of culture) — a distinction that still organises media analysis, from
    interface icons to documentary evidence.
  </p>
  <p>
    The analytical power arrives with the second layer. Roland Barthes distinguished
    <strong>denotation</strong> (the literal sign: this is a photograph of a man in a uniform)
    from <strong>connotation</strong> (the cultural freight: authority, safety, the state) —
    and <em>myth</em>, his term for the way connotations harden into natural-seeming truths.
    This is the microscope of communication research, and advertising is its laboratory: the
    product's signifier (a car) is deliberately chained to connotations (freedom, virility,
    responsibility) that the object itself does not possess — meaning manufactured by
    association, renewed by repetition. News photographs, brand logos, political vocabularies
    ("reform", "threat", "reform-minded") all run on the same machinery. Semiotics explains
    <em>how</em> the manager's "deprioritised" did its four-directional work: the signifier is
    stable, but each listener activated different codes — career experience, past reorganisations,
    rumours — and meaning was assembled per listener, in context. Codes, not objects, carry
    the payload.
  </p>

  <h2>Medium and message: the form is an argument</h2>
  <p>
    Marshall McLuhan's 1964 provocation — <em>the medium is the message</em> — survives as a
    research programme because it is half right in an important way: the <em>form</em> of a
    medium reshapes what communication can be, independently of any particular content. Harold
    Innis had argued that media have biases — stone is time-binding, papyrus space-binding,
    and empires rise on their media; McLuhan radicalised it: print fostered linear thought,
    private reading and nationalism; television fostered spectacle and simultaneity; each
    medium reorganises the sensorium and the social. Walter Ong's history of orality and
    literacy gives the concrete version: cultures without writing remember and argue
    differently — formulaic, rhythmic, situational — because the medium's affordances shape
    thought's habits. The modern analytic vocabulary is <strong>affordances</strong>: a
    medium's persistence (a covenant whispered versus a letter kept for a century),
    replicability (a post screenshot in seconds), scalability, searchability and editability —
    the social physics that differ even when "the message survives". Our manager's sentence
    read aloud in a meeting, sent as an email and leaked as a screenshot are three different
    communication events wearing the same words; the screenshot cannot be walked back, the
    meeting could be. Platform studies — virality, algorithmic curation, the economics of
    attention — are McLuhan's idea with datasets attached.
  </p>
  <p>
    The honest caveat keeps the theory scientific: <strong>technological determinism</strong>
    — the claim that media <em>cause</em> social forms — overstates the case. Societies shape
    their media as much as media shape societies (the same television served totalitarian and
    democratic projects); McLuhan's aphorism works best as a strong hypothesis about
    affordances, not a law of history. The same discipline applies to effects research, which
    has swung across a century from the "hypodermic needle" (messages injected into passive
    audiences) through "limited effects" to agenda setting (McCombs and Shaw's 1972 finding
    that media tell us what to think <em>about</em>, if not what to think), framing (how the
    telling is done), cultivation and the <strong>spiral of silence</strong> (Noelle-Neumann:
    people mute minority opinions they perceive as unpopular, and the silence feeds the
    perception) — a lineage this unit's descendants will trace in detail.
  </p>

  <h2>Going deeper</h2>
  <p>
    One worked analysis shows the spectrum doing analytical labour on a single message. Take
    a government's pandemic slogan — "stay home, protect the NHS, save lives" (the United
    Kingdom's 2020 formulation, which became one of the most-repeated campaigns of the
    century). Read as <em>transmission</em>, the question is reach and fidelity: did the
    words arrive, was recall high? (They did and it was.) Read as <em>meaning-centred</em>,
    the questions multiply: what does "stay home" mean to a flat-dweller with children, to a
    gig worker deciding between rent and quarantine, to someone whose home is not safe? Read
    <em>semiotically</em>, the tricolon binds three signifiers — private act, national
    institution, universal value — into a chain that shifts responsibility toward the
    individual; critics read that chain, supporters read solidarity. Read as
    <em>medium</em>: the slogan's rhythm was engineered for broadcast repetition and then
    detonated by social media's remix affordances — parodied, photoshopped, protested — the
    same words travelling through channels with different social physics. And read as
    <em>constitutive</em>: the slogan did not describe a shared national effort, it was one
    of the practices <em>making</em> that effort — and its reception differences marked the
    communities being constituted differently by it. No single tradition owns the case; the
    spectrum is the analysis, and the analyst's first professional decision is always which
    question — delivery, meaning, form or constitution — the case at hand is actually about.
  </p>
  <p>
    Three threads carry the foundation into current research. <strong>Encoding/decoding</strong>
    (Stuart Hall, 1973) supplies the field's bridge from semiotics to audience: producers
    <em>encode</em> preferred meanings, but audiences may read dominantly, negotiate, or read
    <em>oppositionally</em> — the same broadcast producing consent, irony or hostility in
    different living rooms, which is why effects and meanings must be studied as encounters,
    not deliveries. <strong>The constitutive programme</strong> has become organisational and
    institutional theory's language: organisations "exist" as communication (meetings, memos,
    onboarding rituals, the org chart as a communication artefact), and identity — personal
    and corporate — is performed in interaction; the manager's sentence did not describe the
    team's status, it <em>was</em> the team's status being made. <strong>The algorithmic
    turn</strong> is the live frontier: when a recommender system selects which messages reach
    whom, "the channel" acquires agency that Shannon never modelled — virality, filter bubbles,
    misinformation dynamics and AI-generated content all press on the field's oldest
    distinctions (source, message, channel, noise) and sometimes dissolve them (who is the
    sender when a model drafts and an algorithm targets?). Craig's map of seven traditions
    proves newly useful here: cybernetic (systems and information), semiotic (signs and codes),
    socio-psychological (effects and persuasion), sociocultural (meaning in communities),
    critical (power in the system), phenomenological (lived experience) and rhetorical (the
    art of influence) each grab a piece of the algorithmic question — and the founding
    spectrum, from pipe to constitutive, is still the discipline's best map of which piece
    each theory is actually holding.
  </p>

  <p class="sources"><strong>Sources:</strong> NCA learning outcomes; Shannon &amp; Weaver, <em>The Mathematical Theory of Communication</em> (1948); McLuhan, <em>Understanding Media</em> (1964); Craig, "Communication Theory as a Field" (1999).</p>
